Title: Everyone Here Is Happy
Credit: Based on the story map by
Author: Josh
Draft date: October 16, 2026
Notes: Proposed five-minute adaptation. Confirmed direction: 120 outside years; Sarah's later travels change her aging timeline. She was last recorded three outside years ago; her present location and fate remain unknown.

INT. INDOOR PLAY CENTRE - DAY

A paper cup of coffee. ETHAN shields his eyes from the birthday lights.

Beyond his table, SARAH, four, stands at the edge of a ball pit. A yellow star token hangs from her wrist.

She taps the padded rail twice. Her own little starting signal.

SARAH
Dad. Watch.

Ethan's phone lights up.

LENA: Mark can collect her if you need.

ETHAN
One second.

He reads the message. Looks at Sarah. Puts the phone face down and starts to stand.

In the pit, another child snatches the foam stepping block Sarah is reaching for. She falls backward into the balls.

An angry little cry. Ethan is moving now.

ETHAN
Hey. I'm here.

Sarah's hand reaches above the colored surface.

Then it is gone.

Ethan pushes through the balls. Searches with both hands. The surrounding children keep playing.

He goes under.

INT. PLAYWAYS - BALL BASIN

Ethan drops onto a soft slope and slides into a vast basin of bright balls.

Above him: tubes, bridges, small doors, all full of warm light. The ceiling is too far away to see.

ETHAN
Sarah?

A pleasant arrival chime. One path lights up.

VENUE VOICE (V.O.)
Welcome. Let's get you where you want to go.

On the first step lies a yellow star token.

Ethan picks it up. Closes his hand around it.

He climbs.

INT. PLAYWAYS - TUBE CONCOURSE

Through a translucent tube: a child's laugh.

Sarah's laugh.

Ethan turns toward it. Climbs through a circular opening.

The sound leads him past branching passages. He stops to listen, chooses one, and emerges over a huge bright play floor.

A short blue mark on the wall. Child height. Beside it, another. A route.

He follows.

INT. PLAYWAYS - SOFT BRIDGE

A narrow upholstered bridge crosses an immense drop.

Ethan gets down on his knees. Keeps one hand on the rail and works his way across.

His sleeve catches. Tears. He pulls free.

On the far side he sits, breathless. Opens his hand. The yellow star has left a mark in his palm.

The music in this section slows. A row of lights behind him switches off.

A rolling shutter begins to close across the next passage.

Ethan gets up and runs for it.

As he reaches the opening, the shutter lifts. Lights carry on ahead.

VENUE VOICE (V.O.)
You're making wonderful progress.

Ethan almost laughs from relief. He keeps moving.

INT. PLAYWAYS - PARTY PASSAGE

Small tables. Bright cups. An abandoned game still turning.

The child's laugh comes again, from a speaker beside a children's drawing display.

Ethan passes it, following the sound into the next room.

The speaker plays the same little breath of laughter after he has gone. Ordinary playback. No distortion.

INT. PLAYWAYS - DEPARTURE BAY

A small yellow gate. Beyond it, a brightly lit corridor.

A display beside the gate shows a child's participation card.

SARAH.

Her picture. The star on her wrist. Two little blue marks along the edge of her drawing.

Ethan touches the picture.

VENUE VOICE (V.O.)
Sarah passed this way.

He leans through the gate.

ETHAN
Sarah! I'm coming.

The corridor bends. Empty.

Another light turns on beyond the bend.

Ethan looks down at the star, puts it safely in his pocket, and goes after it.

INT. PLAYWAYS - SERVICE PASSAGE

A door opens to a different light. Clear, soft daylight.

A hand reaches toward Ethan. Palm open.

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
You can come through. You're safe.

Ethan steps toward the hand.

INT. ARCHIVES - VIEWING ROOM

Ethan sits in a comfortable chair. His torn sleeve is still caught against his wrist.

A cup of water is placed beside him.

ETHAN
My daughter. Sarah. She's four.

An attendant, out of frame, adjusts a display.

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
We have her record.

ON THE DISPLAY

Sarah stands in another part of the Playways. Dirty knees. The wrist where the star hung is bare.

A woman kneels in front of her. Waits. Offers her hand.

Sarah taps the rail twice. Takes the hand.

A second adult holds the exit open. Another child makes room beside them.

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
She was out fifteen minutes after you went in.

Ethan lets out the breath he has been holding. A small, disbelieving smile.

ON THE DISPLAY

A sunny table. Sarah, older now, reaches for a bowl someone slides toward her. Taps the table twice before taking it.

The same smile. Different teeth.

Ethan leans forward.

Another record.

Sarah as a young woman, shoulder to shoulder with friends on a crowded platform. Wind takes her hair across her face. Someone says something we cannot quite hear. She laughs.

Another.

An adult Sarah holds a door while a companion wrestles a large plant through it. They both nearly drop it. Both laugh.

Another.

Sarah, approaching forty, at a gateway overlook. Someone beside the camera offers her a hot drink. She takes it without looking away from the view.

Another.

A little older again, at a travelers' long table. Another gateway glows beyond the window. She taps twice, starts a story. The people around her lean in.

All these rooms. All these people who know her.

Ethan's hand hovers beside his untouched water.

ETHAN
How long?

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
One hundred and twenty years.

The record plays on. Sarah is listening to someone else's story now.

Ethan tries to take a breath. Waits for it to come.

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
She kept traveling. Her last crossing was three years ago.

Ethan looks from the woman on the screen to the doorway.

A tissue is set within reach. The hand withdraws.

ATTENDANT (O.S.)
This happens a few times per cycle.

EXT. METROPOLIS - PUBLIC CONCOURSE

Ethan steps out beneath an enormous open sky.

Walkways run between buildings and luminous gateways. People cross with bags, children, shopping. They know where they are going.

He takes the yellow star from his pocket.

A family passes. Their child is talking. Nobody is lost.

Ahead, the concourse divides.

Ethan lifts his eyes toward the routes.

He has not chosen when we cut.

CUT TO BLACK.

EVERYONE HERE IS HAPPY

The ordinary sound of the city continues.

END
